# 65: a `refer()` behind a CONJUNCT is invisible to the entity graph,
# so `reaches` answers `unreachable` for a link the model states and
# `relations` checks nothing over it. Both ports agree, so this is a
# shared defect rather than a parity break.
#
# run: node ../../../ts/bin/aontu.js reaches x pp refer-behind-conjunct-invisible.aon
# run: aontu-go reaches x pp refer-behind-conjunct-invisible.aon
#
# expected: verdict: reaches   (a.link is ["pp"], and link is refer()-typed)
# actual (both ports): verdict: unreachable, exit 1
#
# Remove the `unique() &` and the same document answers `reaches`,
# exit 0, in both ports -- the companion file is that one-token
# difference.
#
# MINIMISED, via graphOf directly:
#   link: refer() & string                        -> 1 edge
#   link: [&: refer() & string]                   -> 1 edge
#   link: unique() & [&: refer() & string]        -> 0 edges
#
# So it is the CONJUNCT, not the list and not the spread: graphOf's
# walk does not descend into conjunct arms, so any relation guarded by
# a sizing atom, a constraint or any other `&` term drops out of the
# graph entirely.
#
# THIS IS NOT HYPOTHETICAL SPELLING. use-cases/05-rbac-policy writes
#   grants: unique() & [&: refer() & string]
# which is the natural way to say "a set of grants, no duplicates". On
# that model graphOf reports 13 entities and ZERO edges, and
#   aontu reaches admin admin_all
# answers `unreachable` although roles.admin.grants is ["admin_all"].
# The case's own check.sh does not exercise `reaches`, which is why
# nothing caught it.
p: id(pp) & {n: 1}
a: id(x) & {link: unique() & [&: refer() & string], link: ["pp"]}
